OpenTTD 14.1 for RISC OS
========================

OpenTTD is an open source transport simulation game based on
Transport Tycoon Deluxe. This is a RISC OS port of OpenTTD 14.1.

RISC OS port (C) 2026 Andrew Youll. The application sprite is from the
2005 RISC OS port by David Llewellyn-Jones.
OpenTTD is (C) the OpenTTD team and is licensed under the GNU GPL v2
(see docs.COPYING).


Requirements
------------
 * RISC OS 5 on an ARMv7 or later machine with VFP, for example a
   Raspberry Pi 2, 3, 4 or 400. It will NOT run on ARMv6 machines
   (Pi 1 / Zero), older ARM machines or RPCEmu.
 * The ARMEABISupport module.
 * SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later.
 * For sound, the SharedSound (1.07 or later), StreamManager (0.03 or
   later) and SharedSoundBuffer (0.07 or later) modules. !Run loads
   them from System:Modules if they're there. Without them the
   DigitalRenderer module is used instead, and with neither the game
   runs silently. Music needs sound to be working.
 * About 128MB of free memory.

You can install ARMEABISupport and SharedUnixLibrary with !PackMan.


Sound modules
-------------
StreamManager and SharedSoundBuffer are freeware by John Duffell. His
terms say they may not be published on other web sites, so they are not
included with OpenTTD. If you don't have them, download ssb.zip from the
!RDPClient page on Andrew Sellors' website, where they are hosted by kind
permission of the author:

  https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/

then merge its !System into yours: open !Boot, click System, and drag
the !System from ssb.zip into the System merge window.

John Duffell's own site (now on the Internet Archive) has more details:

  https://web.archive.org/web/20110920080106/http://www.duffell.riscos.me.uk/


Running
-------
Double-click !OpenTTD. The game can run full screen or in a window on
the desktop. Both are set in Game Options > Graphics:

 * Full screen: tick it to take over the whole screen, untick it to
   play in a desktop window (you can switch while the game is running).
 * Resolution: when full screen, this picks the screen mode and RISC OS
   switches to it; when windowed, it sets the size of the window.

Smaller resolutions or windows run faster, for example 1280x720 or
1024x768. Your desktop mode is restored when you leave full screen or
quit. In a window, click in the window to give it the keyboard.
While windowed the game has an icon bar icon: click it to bring the
window to the front, or use Menu > Quit. Switching to full screen
suspends the desktop until you go back to a window or quit.

By default !Run opens the game in a 1024x768 desktop window. To change
this, edit these lines in !Run:

  Set OpenTTD$Display windowed
  Set OpenTTD$Size 1024x768

Use "windowed" or "fullscreen" and the size you want, or put a | in
front of both lines to use the game's own Graphics settings instead.

If your desktop uses a high resolution mode (EX0 EY0, sometimes called
180dpi), the window is drawn at double size so the game isn't tiny. There's
a line in !Run to turn this off or choose a different scale.

The free OpenGFX graphics are included, so you don't need the original
Transport Tycoon files. The OpenSFX sounds come in a second archive
(OpenTTD-14.1-riscos-OpenSFX.zip); unpack it over !OpenTTD to add them.

If you have the original Transport Tycoon Deluxe files, you can copy them
into your OpenTTD choices directory (see below) and choose them from the
game options.

The music comes in a third archive (OpenTTD-14.1-riscos-Music.zip);
unpack it over !OpenTTD too. It has the OpenMSX music and a General MIDI
SoundFont (TimGM6mb) to play it with, because RISC OS has no MIDI
synthesiser of its own. If you have !MIDISynth installed, its SoundFont
is used instead. Music needs sound to be working.


Where things are saved
----------------------
Settings, saved games, screenshots and downloaded content are kept in

  <Choices$Write>.OpenTTD

Messages from the game go into <Wimp$ScrapDir>.OpenTTDlog. If something
goes wrong, it can also record how much memory the game is using every
10 seconds: remove the | from the "Set OpenTTD$Debug" line in !Run.


Two builds
----------
There are two copies of the game in !OpenTTD: openttd-fast uses the NEON
instructions of the Raspberry Pi 2 and later (and other Cortex-A
machines), and openttd is the standard build. !Run uses openttd-fast when
it's there. To use the standard one, put a | in front of the IfThere line
in !Run.

To see where the time goes, open the Frame rate window from the help
menu (the "?" button on the toolbar).


Options
-------
The sprite drawer (the "blitter") is chosen by the line
Set OpenTTD$Blitter in !Run:

  32bpp-optimized  the default
  32bpp-anim       animated water and lights (slower)
  32bpp-neon       draws sprites with the NEON instructions of the
                   Raspberry Pi 2 and later (openttd-fast only). It is
                   a port of the SSE sprite drawer that PCs use, and
                   draws exactly the same picture. Experimental: try it
                   and compare the Frame rate window. It keeps sprites
                   uncompressed, so it uses more memory.

You can also edit the Run line in !Run to change how the game starts:

  -v sdl:threads   draw the screen from a separate thread (off by
                   default on RISC OS)
  -s null          turn sound off
  -m null          turn music off (instead of <OpenTTD$Music>)


Source code
-----------
This build uses the unmodified OpenTTD 14.1 source from
https://github.com/OpenTTD/OpenTTD (tag 14.1), plus the small RISC OS
patch in docs.riscos-patch. It was built with the GCCSDK GCC 10.2
cross compiler and SDL 2.26 (RISC OS video driver).

